%I A401000 #14 Oct 19 2026 00:25:04
%S A401000 1,2,8,61,430,3678,31799,284300,2566885,23443619
%N A401000 Number of free left-handed polymarquises with n cells.
%C A401000 See A400998 for details.
%C A401000 Since the neighbor relation is not invariant under reflection, it may not be as natural to define free left-handed polymarquises as in other cases of free polyforms.
%C A401000 Say that a (one-sided) left-handed polymarquis is flippable if its mirror image is still connected by left-handed marquis moves. A left-handed polymarquis and its mirror image are counted as distinct one-sided forms (A400999) precisely if they are flippable and chiral (as a set of square cells). In the present sequence the two forms in such a pair are identified and counted only once, so the number of flippable, chiral pairs of forms equals A400999(n)-a(n). Since most left-handed polymarquises are not flippable, the ratio A400999(n)/a(n) between one-sided and free forms tends to 1, as opposed to many other cases where the corresponding ratio tends to 2.
%H A401000 Pontus von Brömssen, <a href="/A401000/a401000.svg">The 23 = A400999(5)-a(5) chiral, flippable, lefthanded pentamarquises (one from each chiral pair is shown)</a>.
%H A401000 <a href="/index/Pol#polyominoes">Index entries for sequences related to polyominoes</a>.
%e A401000 For n = 4, there are 2 flippable, chiral pairs of left-handed tetramarquises (corresponding to the 2 chiral tetrominoes), so a(4) = A400999(4)-2 = 61.
%e A401000 For n = 5, there are 23 flippable, chiral pairs of left-handed pentamarquises (see linked illustration), so a(5) = A400999(5)-23 = 430.
%Y A401000 Cf. A400404, A400998 (fixed), A400999 (one-sided).
%K A401000 nonn,more,changed
%O A401000 1,2
%A A401000 _Pontus von Brömssen_, Oct 15 2026
